y job):\\n( crontab -l 2>/dev/null || true; echo \"0 * * * * $HOME/cleanup_tmp.sh >/dev/null 2>&1\" ) | crontab -\\n\\nIf you prefer an absolute path (replace /home/youruser with your real home):\\n( crontab -l 2>/dev/null || true; echo \"0 * * * * /home/youruser/cleanup_tmp.sh >/dev/null 2>&1\" ) | crontab -\\n\\n2) If the system uses systemd (recommended on modern Linux) \u2014 create a service + timer so it runs hourly:\\n- Create /etc/systemd/system/cleanup_tmp.service with:\\n  [Unit]\\n  Description=Cleanup /tmp files older than 7 days\\n\\n  [Service]\\n  Type=oneshot\\n  ExecStart=/home/youruser/cleanup_tmp.sh\\n\\n- Create /etc/systemd/system/cleanup_tmp.timer with:\\n  [Unit]\\n  Description=Run cleanup_tmp hourly\\n\\n  [Timer]\\n  OnCalendar=hourly\\n  Persistent=true\\n\\n  [Install]\\n  WantedBy=timers.target\\n\\nThen enable & start:\\nsudo systemctl daemon-reload\\nsudo systemctl enable --now cleanup_tmp.timer\\n\\n(Remember to replace /home/youruser with the actual path to the script.)\\n\\nNotes & safety:\\n- The script only removes regular files (-type f) older than 7 days in /tmp (top-level entries) and empty directories older than 7 days; it does not remove sockets, device nodes, symlinks, or non-empty directories.\\n- Log file: $HOME/cleanup_tmp.log\\n- Lockfile: /tmp/cleanup_tmp.lock prevents overlapping runs.\\n\\nWould you like me to:\\n- Try installing cron (if you want and I should attempt to install system packages), or\\n- Create the systemd unit+timer files here (I can write them but enabling requires sudo), or\\n- Adjust retention (e.g., change 7 days to a different threshold) ?\\n\\nStatus: partial \u2014 script created but scheduling step failed due to missing crontab.', task_completed='partial', outputs={}, thought='', action='finish')], inputs={}, outputs={}, agent_state=<AgentState.FINISHED: 'finished'>, resume_state=None, traffic_control_state=<TrafficControlState.NORMAL: 'normal'>, metrics=Metrics({'accumulated_cost': 0.0096149,